Subject: [PATCH 0006] Let a single-threaded port declare thread-locals as globals
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl), with -D_Py_THREAD_LOCAL_AS_GLOBAL
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

CPython keeps the current thread state in a C11 thread-local, _Py_tss_tstate
(Python/pystate.c), which pycore_pystate.h reads inline in nearly every core
file, and one more in Python/import.c. capstone64 cannot lower thread-local
storage: isel fails with "Cannot select: c128 = GlobalTLSAddress", which is
registry issue C-47 ("what this issue still blocks: ... any application source
that declares thread-local variables").

A domain runs on one hart and has no clone, so a thread-local has exactly one
instance, and a global is that instance. _Py_THREAD_LOCAL_AS_GLOBAL makes
_Py_thread_local expand to nothing; HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL stays 1, so the code
paths are the ones CPython takes with TLS. It is opt-in: without the define
nothing changes. It must not be set for any build that can start a thread, and
it becomes unnecessary when C-47 is resolved.

--- a/Include/pyport.h
+++ b/Include/pyport.h
@@ -484,7 +484,11 @@
 #      error "HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL is already defined"
 #    endif
 #    define HAVE_THREAD_LOCAL 1
-#    ifdef thread_local
+#    if defined(_Py_THREAD_LOCAL_AS_GLOBAL)
+       // A process that can never run a second thread: one instance of a
+       // thread-local IS a global. Set by a port, never by default.
+#      define _Py_thread_local
+#    elif defined(thread_local)
 #      define _Py_thread_local thread_local
 #    elif __STDC_VERSION__ >= 201112L && !defined(__STDC_NO_THREADS__)
 #      define _Py_thread_local _Thread_local
